**D2 -- REFINE (disposition right, record wrong-membered).** That no documented guest observes the revert target is DERIVED from the packet's own quotes: NetBSD reads the command port only immediately after writing poll, and its IRR/ISR-reading branch is `#else`'d out (READ, 1.7); Linux/malta never writes OCW3 0x0c at all (READ, 1.8). So disclosure over edit is right. But every other approximation in this fix received a FILED NAME (`i8259ocw2nonspec`, `i8259ltim`, ...) and the revert target received only this pass's prose; it will be lost when the disclosure scroll archives. File it (e.g. `i8259ocw3revert`) alongside `i8259ocw2nonspec`. Answer to the question: no supported guest observes it; it is a fidelity row, not a correctness debt.

**D3 -- REFINE (ruling substance UPHOLD; warrant mis-cited).** UPHOLD the line: HEAD's convention is the line (READ -- bus_isa.c:87 sets `last_int` to the scan line; the GT arm returns it), and a functioning malta-Linux timer loop under HEAD -- thousands of delivery-EOI cycles in the storm measurements -- DERIVES that the guest consumes the line convention successfully; a diff that returned `irq_base + n` would change what a working guest sees unless base = 0. Answers: (a) "does any GT-IACK-reading guest program a non-zero ICW2 base?" NOT CHECKABLE -- Linux's ICW2 programming is not inlined for either guest (only ICW1 is); the settling fetch is `drivers/irqchip/irq-i8259.c`'s ICW2 base plus `malta-int.c`'s dispatch line, inlined. (b) If the honest form is `irq_base + n`, it is a **filed row, not a diff change** -- the divergence predates the diff and the diff's convention is HEAD's by construction.

**D4 -- UPHOLD.** I walked the inlined code independently: master ack clears pic1 IRR bit 2 / sets ISR bit 2 / recompute DEASSERTs the CPU line; slave ack takes pic2 bit 0 (line 8), leaves 0x02; pic2's recompute ASSERTs ISA line 2 -> `isa_interrupt_assert(2)` samples `old_isa_assert` with bit 2 already clear (the packet's subtle claim, verified), re-folds bit 2 from pic2's 0x02, scan skips x==2 and breaks at x=9 -> `last_int`=9, CPU line re-asserted. No stranded bit, no stall (all DERIVED from the inlined verbatim code). Caveats kept: it is a second derivation, not a measurement -- the owed `i8259cascline` row should land WITH the diff; and the spurious-cascade value (n2 = -1 -> odata = 2, guest dispatches "line 2" where the chip would return the slave's spurious vector) is unpinned and unfiled; half a line under `i8259cascline`.

**D5 -- REFINE.** The derivation is right against *deterministic* rows and wrong against rows: the "same visible effect" sentence fails in the count>=2 case the main loop itself identifies -- with IRQ 0 masked, two host fires, one slave acknowledge, then unmask, the mutant's wrong decrement erases a backlog event that the count-gated re-fold should re-deliver (that the re-fold triggers on count>0 is DERIVED from HEAD's own storm: the EOI consumed IRR bit 0 yet re-delivery continued). A band row is constructible inside the probe's existing long-wait precedent: mask IRQ 0, wait (count reaches >=2), acknowledge the RTC on IRQ 8, unmask, count deliveries over a window -- correct >=2, mutant <=1; the only non-determinism is the wait, which the rate row's own band precedent absorbs. Premise to check: that the mc146818 model supports a usable periodic rate (gtboth proves the probe drives it; its range is unstated -- NOT CHECKABLE). Either row it, or correct the record to "rowable as band, declined on cost" -- "no deterministic row exists" is true, "no row" is not. Note also: this row is the same instrument as the missing rate floor (Q3.1).

